id: design.cache-invalidation
kind: design
title: Cache invalidation
aliases: invalidation, cache coherence, stale data
tags: caching, reliability
questions:
  - how do you invalidate a cache correctly?
invariant: Every cache entry has a defined lifetime and a defined invalidation trigger; staleness is bounded by configuration, not by hope.
answer_30s: Invalidate explicitly on writes and always keep a TTL as the backstop, and decide per key whether stale-but-fast is acceptable -- the answer differs for a price and for a session.
answer_2m: |
  Three mechanisms, usually all three: TTL (bounds staleness without any
  coordination), key-versioning (embed a version or content hash in the key, so
  a schema or format change silently bypasses old entries), and pub/sub
  invalidation for values that must be refreshed immediately (broadcast the key,
  each replica drops its local copy). Multi-layer caches compound the problem:
  CDN, reverse proxy, application cache and database cache each need a story,
  and purge propagation is the part that actually breaks in production.
  Versioned cache keys are the most robust modern answer because they make
  invalidation a deployment concern rather than a runtime race.
when_to_use:
  - any cache; the question is which mechanism per key, not whether
avoid_when:
  - nothing: a cache without an invalidation plan is a correctness bug waiting for a deploy
failure_modes:
  - invalidation on write that races with a concurrent read, re-populating the stale value (fixed by versioned keys)
  - only TTL, so users see stale data for the whole window after a correction
  - forgotten layer (CDN or client) serving the old value after a purge
related:
  - design.cache-aside
  - design.redis-cache
  - rust.tokio-broadcast
  - rust.tokio-watch
  - recipe.cache-backed-api
sources:
  - https://redis.io/docs/manual/client-side-caching/
